    "description": "Tharu Embroidery received its GI tag in 2023. This traditional craft originates from the Tharu tribal community in the Terai region of Uttar Pradesh, particularly in districts like Bareilly, Lakhimpur Kheri and Bahraich. Known for its vibrant colors and intricate patterns, Tharu Embroidery reflects the cultural identity and heritage of the Tharu tribe.",
    "history": "Tharu Embroidery has been practiced by the indigenous Tharu community of the Terai region — the strip of forested land at the foot of the Himalayas along the Nepal border — for centuries. The embroidery is traditionally done by women on garments, household textiles, and ritual items, with patterns passed down orally and through practice from mother to daughter.",
    "uniqueness": "Tharu Embroidery is distinguished by its vibrant geometric patterns, extensive use of tiny mirrors (shisha work), and motifs depicting animals, birds, trees, and scenes from tribal life. The colour combinations — typically red, black, and white on natural fabric — and the specific geometric vocabulary of the Tharu people reflect a cultural identity unique to the Himalayan foothills communities.",
    "makingProcess": "Tharu women embroider on coarsely woven cotton fabric using coloured cotton thread and small round mirrors (shisha). Designs are worked freehand from memory using running stitch, chain stitch, and cross-stitch techniques. Mirrors are attached by working a series of interlocking stitches around the rim of each mirror to hold it in place. Finished textiles are used for garments, wall hangings, and household textiles.",
    "materials": [],
    "artisanName": "Maneesha, Geeta RanaDistrict & State: Bareilly, Uttar Pradesh",
    "challenges": "Before GI registration, Tharu Embroidery faced numerous challenges: Lack of formal recognition for the unique tribal art form. Declining interest among younger generations to continue the craft. Limited market exposure and competition from machine-made embroidery. Insufficient financial resources to procure quality materials and modernize tools.",
    "nabardSupport": "NABARD has been instrumental in supporting the preservation and promotion of Tharu Embroidery through assistance with pre-registration documentation, awareness campaigns, and post-registration activities to secure intellectual property rights. Training programs to enhance the skills of Tharu women, enabling them to produce high-quality and market-relevant designs. Sponsorship of exhibitions, fairs, and online platforms to showcase Tharu Embroidery to national and international audiences. Linking artisans with financial institutions for credit and grants to improve production and scale their businesses.",
    "impact": "The GI tag has brought renewed attention to Tharu Embroidery, helping preserve the cultural heritage of the Tharu community. It has improved the livelihoods of artisans by creating sustainable income opportunities and promoting the art form globally."
