    "description": "Bareilly Zari Zardozi Bareilly Zari Zardozi received its GI tag This intricate embroidery technique involves using gold or silver threads on fabric and embellishing with sequins. The craft has its origins in Persia and was brought to India in the 16th century.",
    "history": "Zardozi embroidery was brought to India from Persia during the Mughal period. Artisans following Mughal patronage settled in Bareilly in the Rohilkhand region, where the craft flourished under the nawabs of Rohilkhand. Bareilly developed its own distinct Zardozi tradition, and the craft received its GI tag in recognition of this regional heritage.",
    "uniqueness": "Bareilly Zari Zardozi uses real gold and silver metallic threads along with sequins, pearls, and metal chips to create heavily embossed three-dimensional embroidery on silk and velvet. The craft is distinguished from machine-made imitations by the depth and irregularity of the hand-stitched raised work, which gives each piece a textured, sculptural quality.",
    "makingProcess": "Fabric is stretched taut on a wooden embroidery frame (adda). The artisan draws or traces the design pattern onto the fabric. Using a hook-like needle (ari), the craftsman pulls fine metallic thread from below the fabric to the surface in a chain-stitch motion, while simultaneously incorporating sequins, glass beads, pearls, and metal shapes. Building up the design in layers creates the characteristic three-dimensional raised effect.",
