    "description": "received its GI tag in 2023. This exquisite craft originates from Sambhal, Uttar Pradesh, and involves creating intricate designs and utility items using camel and buffalo bone. The craft showcases the skilled artistry of local artisans, passed down through generations, and is recognized for its fine detailing and aesthetic appeal.",
    "history": "Bone craft in Sambhal district has been practiced for centuries, with the artisan community developing expertise in carving and inlaying the horns and bones of naturally deceased animals. The craft is closely related to the town's ivory-carving tradition (which predated restrictions on ivory), with craftsmen transitioning to bone when ivory became unavailable.",
    "uniqueness": "Sambhal Bone Craft uses only bones of naturally deceased animals — primarily bovine — which are carved, drilled, and inlaid to create jewellery, combs, decorative panels, and utility objects. The natural creamy-white tones and grain patterns of bone give each piece a distinct organic character, and the fine relief carving technique developed in Sambhal has no equivalent elsewhere in India.",
    "makingProcess": "Bones are cleaned thoroughly, bleached in sunlight, and dried until completely moisture-free. They are cut into flat sheets or strips using fine-toothed saws. The design is transferred to the bone surface with a pencil or carbon tracing. Craftsmen use files, chisels, engraving burins, and drills to carve and pierce the design. Inlay work uses contrasting materials such as brass wire or resin. The finished pieces are polished with fine abrasive paper to a smooth ivory-like finish.",
    "materials": [],
    "artisanName": "Mehandi HasanDistrict & State: Sambhal, Uttar Pradesh",
    "challenges": "Before GI registration, Sambhal Bone Craft faced several challenges: Lack of Recognition: The unique craftsmanship was overshadowed by cheaper imitations from other regions. Ethical Misunderstandings: Misconceptions about the craft's raw material usage led to negative perceptions and restricted market access. Decline in Demand: Limited exposure to national and international markets reduced the profitability of the craft.",
    "nabardSupport": "NABARD has significantly contributed to the preservation and promotion of Sambhal Bone Craft: GI Registration Support: Assistance with documentation, awareness campaigns, and ensuring proper legal protection of the craft. Skill Development Programs: Conducted training workshops to improve artisans' techniques and encourage innovative product designs. Market Linkages: Supported artisans by facilitating participation in national and international exhibitions and fairs.",
    "impact": "The GI tag has enhanced the reputation of Sambhal Bone Craft, increasing demand for authentic products. It has boosted the livelihoods of local artisans and helped preserve a unique cultural and artistic heritage for future generations."
