    "auCount": 0,
    "description": "Banaras Tiranga Barfi Banaras Tiranga Barfi, also known as Kashi's Tiranga Barfi, received its GI tag on April 16, 2024. This tricolored sweet holds historical significance as it played a role in India's freedom movement. The geographical area covered by the GI tag includes Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh.",
    "history": "Banaras Tiranga Barfi has a historical significance dating to India's freedom movement. Local confectioners of Varanasi created this tricolour sweet in the national colours — saffron, white, and green — as a symbol of patriotism. The sweet played a role in freedom movement gatherings and is today consumed widely during national festivals and celebrations.",
    "uniqueness": "Banaras Tiranga Barfi is unique in its three-layered structure, each layer made from a different milk reduction (khoya) formula: saffron-coloured with kesar for the top, plain white in the middle, and green-coloured with pista for the bottom. The specific ratios and cooking methods for each layer, developed by Banarasi halwais, give the sweet a distinctive appearance and flavour profile.",
    "makingProcess": "Khoya (reduced milk) is prepared by slow-cooking full-fat milk in wide pans until almost all moisture evaporates. Three separate batches are prepared: one mixed with kesar and saffron-hued colour, one kept plain white, and one mixed with pista paste and green colour. Each layer is pressed and cooled in sequence in a flat tray, then the set slab is cut into rectangular barfi pieces.",
