    "auCount": 0,
    "description": "received its GI tag on March 30, 2024. This traditional sweet has a history dating back to the British era, about 200 years ago. Known for its unique taste and quality, Jaunpur Imarti has gained popularity both within India and abroad. The geographical area covered includes Jaunpur district of Uttar Pradesh.",
    "history": "Jaunpur Imarti has a history of approximately 200 years, with the sweet's origins traced to the British colonial era when confectioners in Jaunpur developed their distinctive version of the imarti (a urad dal-based fried sweet soaked in sugar syrup). Jaunpur's version became famous for its specific colour, texture, and flavour, distinguishing it from imarti made elsewhere.",
    "uniqueness": "Jaunpur Imarti is distinguished by its characteristic deep amber-orange colour, flower/spiral shape, and a specific ratio of urad dal batter that achieves a simultaneously crispy exterior and soft interior. The prolonged frying process in pure desi ghee and the specific sugar syrup concentration used in Jaunpur produce a texture and flavour that cannot be replicated by following standard imarti recipes.",
    "makingProcess": "Urad dal (split black gram) is soaked for several hours, drained, and ground to a fine, aerated batter. The batter is piped through a cloth or nozzle in a spiral pattern directly into hot pure ghee and fried at a controlled temperature until crisp and golden. The fried imarti is immediately transferred to warm sugar syrup (one-string consistency) and soaked for a few minutes before serving.",
    "materials": [],
    "artisanName": "Muskan Modanwal",
    "challenges": "Before GI registration, Jaunpur Imarti faced issues such as lack of recognition for its unique qualities and competition from similar sweets produced in other regions.",
