    "description": "Kanthi Mala is a sacred beaded necklace traditionally crafted from the wood of the Tulsi (holy basil) plant in the Mathura-Vrindavan region of Uttar Pradesh, a historic centre of Vaishnav devotion. The craft recently received its Geographical Indication (GI) tag. Skilled artisans turn, bore, and string thousands of minutely uniform beads to create the malas worn by devotees and used in daily prayer.",
    "history": "The making of Tulsi Kanthi Mala is deeply tied to the Krishna-devotional culture of the Braj region, where Mathura and Vrindavan have been pilgrimage centres for centuries. Generations of local artisan families have supplied malas to temples, ashrams, and pilgrims, sustaining a craft inseparable from the area's religious identity.",
    "uniqueness": "Authentic Kanthi Mala is made entirely from seasoned Tulsi wood, hand-turned into minutely uniform beads and strung without machine assistance. The use of sacred Tulsi wood, the fineness of the beadwork, and the Braj devotional context distinguish the genuine Mathura product from ordinary bead necklaces.",
    "makingProcess": "Seasoned Tulsi wood is cut into small segments, shaped and rounded into beads on a hand lathe, and bored through the centre. The beads are graded for uniformity, polished, and strung in traditional counts such as 108 to form the finished mala.",
